{{!-- ONE ENCOUNTER, wherever it is rendered. Two lists print this: the live one at the top of the
      tab and the Completed fold under it, and the card is the SAME card in both — everything a GM
      gathered stays gathered, stays openable, stays deployable and stays droppable-into after it
      has been run. What changes between the two is one button at the foot of the body (and the
      tick that stands in for the grip), and both change off `used`, which is the very flag the
      context split the lists by (gm-encounters-tab.js): the card can therefore never disagree with
      the list it was printed into, and no caller has to pass a "which list am I in" parameter
      down. That matters more than it looks — Handlebars MERGES a partial's hash into the caller's
      context, so a parameter here would ALSO be a bare lookup against the encounter's own fields,
      and this partial is rendered inside an `{{#each}}` over a view-model built in JS.
      section-heading.hbs states the rule at length.

      A SIBLING OF THE SITE CARD, not a form. Warm paper, a soft shadow and an accent edge down the
      left, as the Sites view one segment over draws its cards; the edge is slate here, the road's
      teal on an expedition and green once a card is filed. The open body is a prep SHEET — notes,
      then what was gathered, one ruled line per thing — not a stack of labelled boxes.

      THE DRAG HANDLE IS THE NAME BUTTON, NOT THE ROW. In Chromium a `draggable` ancestor takes
      the mousedown from every input inside it, so a draggable <li> would leave the per-entry note
      box unselectable — you could not put a caret in it. Narrowing the handle to the
      picture-and-name control puts the note outside the draggable subtree. The button carries
      `data-uuid` and `data-doc-type` because the payload it emits is CORE'S OWN `{type, uuid}`:
      the same thing the sidebar puts on the wire, so dragging a monster out of here onto the
      canvas behaves exactly like dragging it out of the sidebar. An unresolved row is
      `draggable="false"` — there is nothing on the other end of it to drag.

      EVERY <img> HERE CARRIES `draggable="false"`, the opt-out every portrait in the system
      wears (see tests/actors/steading/steading-actor-drag.test.js). The row thumbnail sits INSIDE
      the draggable name button, and without the opt-out Gecko hands over the picture instead of
      the document.

      NO FIELD HERE CARRIES A `name`. Every one of them is written by hand to
      `actor.system.encounters`; a `name` on an input inside this sheet's <form> puts it in core's
      submit data, where the array indices would expand into an OBJECT keyed "0", "1", ... and
      quietly replace the list with it.

      EXPANDING IS A CLASS, NOT A RENDER. The body is emitted for every encounter and hidden by
      `.is-collapsed`, so the caret toggles it in place: instant, and incapable of landing a
      re-render on a note box two rows down that happens to have focus. --}}
<li class="stonetop-gm-encounter{{#if used}} is-used{{/if}}{{#unless open}} is-collapsed{{/unless}}"
    data-encounter-id="{{this.id}}">
	<div class="stonetop-gm-encounter-head stonetop-field-btns">
		{{!-- The grip, and on a filed card a tick in its place until the row is hovered: the fold
		      is read down its left edge, and "done" is the thing to read there. The grip is still
		      the drag handle underneath, so a filed card drags back out exactly as before. --}}
		<span class="stonetop-gm-encounter-grip" draggable="true" aria-hidden="true"
		      data-tooltip="{{localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.encounters.reorder"}}" data-tooltip-direction="UP">
			<i class="fas fa-grip-vertical"></i>{{#if used}}<i class="fas fa-check stonetop-gm-encounter-done-mark"></i>{{/if}}
		</span>
		{{!-- The name IS the input, not a label with a pencil beside it: an encounter gets
		      renamed as often as it gets used ("the bridge" becomes "the bridge at dusk"
		      becomes "the ambush"), and a dialog for a one-line edit is a dialog nobody
		      opens. It reads as a TITLE at rest — no box, no ring — and shows that it is
		      editable on hover (an underline) and on focus (the ring). alt+Up / alt+Down in this
		      box reorders the encounter WITHIN ITS OWN LIST, which is the keyboard half of the
		      grip to its left. --}}
		<input type="text" class="stonetop-gm-encounter-name" value="{{this.name}}" maxlength="120"
		       aria-label="{{localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.encounters.nameLabel"}}">
		{{!-- The head of the note, riding on the row while it is shut (the CSS shows it only under
		      .is-collapsed, where the body it belongs to is hidden). Two bundles both called "the
		      bridge" are told apart by what was written about them, and a column of eight shut
		      cards otherwise says nothing at all. Plain text rather than the enriched HTML below:
		      this is one clipped line, and a content link or a paragraph in it would fight the
		      ellipsis. --}}
		{{#if notesPeek}}<span class="stonetop-gm-encounter-peek">{{this.notesPeek}}</span>{{/if}}
		{{!-- WHAT IS IN IT, BY KIND, and the one thing on the shut row that is about the bundle
		      rather than about the GM's own words. "12 gathered" said how MUCH was here and
		      nothing about what: three monsters and a map read at a glance where twelve of
		      something did not. Built in JS (`groupBundleEntries`), zeroes already dropped, in the
		      fixed order the tally table declares -- so a pill sits in the same place on every
		      card and a column of them is scanned down rather than read across.

		      THE SAME GROUPING THE BODY DRAWS ITS EYEBROWS FROM, read here for its counts and there
		      for its rows. One array and not two passes, so the pills and the lists under them
		      cannot disagree about what an arcanum is.

		      RENDERED ONLY WHEN THERE IS SOMETHING IN IT. An empty encounter drew a "0" that said
		      only what its empty body already says, on the very card a GM is about to drop the
		      first thing onto.

		      THE COUNT COMES BEFORE ITS GLYPH, because that is the order it is read in ("three
		      monsters"). The glyph is `aria-hidden` and the tooltip says the kind in words, so
		      the pill is not a picture nobody can name. --}}
		{{#if groups.length}}
		<span class="stonetop-gm-encounter-count">
			{{#each groups}}
			<span class="stonetop-gm-encounter-tally stonetop-gm-encounter-tally--{{this.key}}"
			      data-tooltip="{{this.tip}}" data-tooltip-direction="UP">{{this.count}}<i class="{{this.icon}}" aria-hidden="true"></i></span>
			{{/each}}
		</span>
		{{/if}}
		{{!-- THE CARET, at the right-hand end of the pills rather than at the head's left. It is
		      the pills a GM reads a shut column by, and the caret belongs to the same question:
		      this row says three monsters and a map, now show me them. It is the one REAL
		      disclosure control (focusable, `aria-expanded`); a click anywhere else on the bare
		      head reaches it too (gm-bundle-tab.js#activateListeners), because on a one-line row
		      the row is what a hand aims at.

		      OUTSIDE the pills' `{{#if}}`, because an encounter with nothing gathered still has a
		      body to open: its notes and the strip that things are dropped onto.

		      The glyph turns -90deg while shut (pointing right, the way every other disclosure in
		      the system turns) off `.is-collapsed` on the <li>. --}}
		<button type="button" class="stonetop-gm-encounter-toggle" aria-expanded="{{#if open}}true{{else}}false{{/if}}"
		        aria-label="{{#if open}}{{localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.encounters.collapse"}}{{else}}{{localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.encounters.expand"}}{{/if}}">
			<i class="fas fa-caret-down"></i>
		</button>
		<span class="stonetop-gm-encounter-tools stonetop-field-btns__tools">
			<button type="button" class="stonetop-gm-encounter-remove"
			        data-tooltip="{{localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.encounters.removeEncounter"}}" data-tooltip-direction="UP">
				<i class="fas fa-trash"></i>
			</button>
		</span>
	</div>

	<div class="stonetop-gm-encounter-body">
		{{!-- THE NOTE COMES FIRST, above the gathered rows. What a GM writes here is what the
		      encounter IS ("they cross at dusk, the ford is watched"); the rows under it are the
		      documents it points at. Reading order follows that, and it puts the note at the same
		      place on every card rather than after a list that is two rows long on one and twelve
		      on the next.

		      A RULED PASSAGE, NOT A BOX. The card is already the frame; a well inside it was a
		      second frame around the one thing on the card that is prose. A rule down its left in
		      the card's accent says "the GM's own words" the way the journals' answers say it, and
		      the pencil parks in its top-right corner, shown on hover.

		      NOT A LIVE `<prose-mirror>`. This sheet re-renders on every prep-page write ANYWHERE in
		      the world, so an editor open on this tab would be torn out from under a GM
		      mid-sentence; the pencil opens a Dialog, which is a window redraw cannot reach (see
		      bundle-notes-dialog.js).

		      AN EMPTY NOTE IS ITS OWN BUTTON. "Add notes…" is one muted line that opens the same
		      dialog, so an untouched card offers somewhere to write without a ruled block of
		      nothing; it wears the pencil's class because it IS the pencil, and the one delegated
		      handler serves both. --}}
		<div class="stonetop-gm-encounter-notes-block{{#unless notesHtml}} stonetop-gm-encounter-notes-block--empty{{/unless}}">
			{{#if notesHtml}}
			<div class="stonetop-gm-encounter-notes">{{{this.notesHtml}}}</div>
			<button type="button" class="stonetop-gm-encounter-notes-edit"
			        data-tooltip="{{localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.encounters.notesEdit"}}" data-tooltip-direction="UP">
				<i class="fas fa-pen"></i>
			</button>
			{{else}}
			<button type="button" class="stonetop-gm-encounter-notes-edit stonetop-gm-encounter-notes-add"
			        data-tooltip="{{localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.encounters.notesEdit"}}" data-tooltip-direction="UP">
				<i class="fas fa-pen" aria-hidden="true"></i> {{localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.encounters.notesAdd"}}
			</button>
			{{/if}}
		</div>

		{{!-- ONE GROUP PER KIND, in the fixed order the tally table declares, and every kind the
		      encounter has none of simply absent. An eyebrow line names it — glyph, small-caps
		      title, count, a hairline running to the edge — and its rows follow. No box round a
		      group: the card is the box, and the eyebrow is enough to tell the monsters from the
		      maps at a glance.

		      THE EYEBROW IS WHAT THE ROWS NO LONGER SAY. A row carries no kind label of its own;
		      under a line that has just said "Monsters and NPCs" it would be the same word twice.
		      Its thumbnail says which one, and its medallion (when it has no picture) repeats the
		      kind only as a glyph.

		      `data-entry-kind` IS LOAD-BEARING, not a styling hook. The reorder gestures read it to
		      answer "could this row go where it was let go of": the body is grouped, so an index in
		      the stored array is no longer a place on screen, and a monster dropped among the maps
		      has nowhere there to land (see `_sameEntryKind` and `nudgeWithinGroup`). The drag
		      handle sends the kind of the group it came out of alongside core's own payload.

		      THE ROWS OF ONE GROUP ARE STILL ONE <ul>, and the keyboard reorder reads exactly that
		      list to find the row above and below. Nest anything else that carries `data-entry-id`
		      inside it and alt+Arrow starts counting rows the GM cannot see. --}}
		{{#each groups}}
		<section class="stonetop-gm-entry-card" data-entry-kind="{{this.key}}">
			<div class="stonetop-gm-entry-card-head">
				<i class="{{this.icon}}" aria-hidden="true"></i>
				<span class="stonetop-gm-entry-card-title">{{this.title}}</span>
				<span class="stonetop-gm-entry-card-count">{{this.count}}</span>
				<span class="stonetop-gm-entry-card-rule" aria-hidden="true"></span>
				{{!-- PUT THEM ALL ON THE MAP, at the end of the line that names them, and only where
				      it can do something: `canDeploy` is set in JS off the group's kind
				      (`groupBundleEntries`), not tested here with a helper, because what the button
				      ACTS on is decided by `_deployEncounter`, which filters on "Actor" itself.

				      IT WEARS ITS WORD. As a bare 16px glyph on a strip it was the one control on
				      the card nobody could name without hovering; "Deploy" says it, and the long
				      form ("put every monster in this encounter on the current map") stays the
				      tooltip. --}}
				{{#if canDeploy}}
				<button type="button" class="stonetop-gm-encounter-deploy"
				        data-tooltip="{{localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.encounters.deploy"}}" data-tooltip-direction="UP">
					{{!-- A MASK, not an <i>. The glyph is game-icons.net art (sbed's "pounce",
					      credited in assets/icons/ATTRIBUTION.md), and worn as a mask over
					      `currentColor` it takes this button's resting ink, its hover and both
					      themes off one file -- where an <img> would need a second copy and a
					      filter to fight. See the tab rail, which wears its whole set this way. --}}
					<span class="stonetop-gm-encounter-deploy-icon" aria-hidden="true"></span>
					<span class="stonetop-gm-encounter-deploy-label">{{localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.encounters.deployShort"}}</span>
				</button>
				{{/if}}
				{{!-- DEPLOY AND FIGHT: the same placing, then the "Start a fight" window with those tokens
				      ticked (gm-encounters-tab.js#deployAndFight). Beside Deploy and only where Deploy
				      is; `canFight` also needs the Fight tab on and a GM reading. Core's own combat glyph,
				      so it reads as the Fight tab's. --}}
				{{#if canFight}}
				<button type="button" class="stonetop-gm-encounter-fight"
				        data-tooltip="{{localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.encounters.deployAndFight"}}" data-tooltip-direction="UP">
					<i class="fa-solid fa-swords" aria-hidden="true"></i>
					<span class="stonetop-gm-encounter-deploy-label">{{localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.encounters.deployAndFightShort"}}</span>
				</button>
				{{/if}}
			</div>
			<ul class="stonetop-gm-encounter-entries">
				{{#each entries}}
				<li class="stonetop-gm-encounter-entry{{#if unresolved}} is-unresolved{{/if}}" data-entry-id="{{this.id}}">
					{{!-- A <button>, not an <a>: an href would make this natively draggable and
					      fight the drag it already emits, and it is a delegated handler either
					      way. See relationships-table.hbs, which says the same thing at length.

					      THE THUMBNAIL RIDES INSIDE IT, so the picture is part of what is grabbed
					      and clicked. `img` is the document's own art (a Scene's `thumb`), with
					      every stock placeholder already turned to "" by `bundleEntryImage`; a row
					      with none wears a round medallion of its kind's glyph, and a row whose
					      target has gone wears a broken link. --}}
					<button type="button" class="stonetop-gm-encounter-entry-open"
					        draggable="{{#if unresolved}}false{{else}}true{{/if}}"
					        data-uuid="{{this.uuid}}" data-doc-type="{{this.type}}"
					        data-tooltip="{{#if unresolved}}{{localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.encounters.entryGoneTip"}}{{else}}{{localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.encounters.entryOpenTip"}}{{/if}}"
					        data-tooltip-direction="UP">
						{{#if unresolved}}<span class="stonetop-gm-entry-thumb stonetop-gm-entry-thumb--glyph" aria-hidden="true"><i class="fas fa-link-slash"></i></span>{{else if img}}<img class="stonetop-gm-entry-thumb" src="{{this.img}}" alt="" draggable="false" loading="lazy">{{else}}<span class="stonetop-gm-entry-thumb stonetop-gm-entry-thumb--glyph" aria-hidden="true"><i class="{{this.icon}}"></i></span>{{/if}}<span class="stonetop-gm-encounter-entry-name">{{this.name}}</span>
					</button>
					<button type="button" class="stonetop-gm-encounter-entry-remove"
					        data-tooltip="{{localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.encounters.removeEntry"}}" data-tooltip-direction="UP">
						<i class="fas fa-xmark"></i>
					</button>
					{{!-- The note reads as TEXT beside the name, not as a field under it: no box, no
					      ring, italic and muted, with a dotted underline on hover and a solid one
					      with full ink once it has the caret. The short placeholder shows only on
					      hover and focus, so an untouched row is a name and nothing else.

					      AFTER THE CROSS IN THE MARKUP, though it is drawn before it: the grid places
					      the three (stonetop.css), and a button straight after a field would be held to
					      the field's height by the field-button rule (tests/styles/field-btns.test.js),
					      which a hover-only cross on a text line has no use for. --}}
					<input type="text" class="stonetop-gm-encounter-entry-note" value="{{this.note}}" maxlength="200"
					       placeholder="{{localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.encounters.entryNotePlaceholder"}}"
					       aria-label="{{localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.encounters.entryNoteLabel"}}">
				</li>
				{{/each}}
			</ul>
		</section>
		{{/each}}

		{{!-- The visible target for "put it at the END". A drop on a row inserts BEFORE
		      that row, which is what keeps the whole router testable without measuring
		      pointer midpoints — and leaves no way to aim at the bottom of the list
		      except this strip. It doubles as the empty encounter's only content. --}}
		<p class="stonetop-gm-encounter-dropzone"><i class="fas fa-arrow-down" aria-hidden="true"></i> {{localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.encounters.dropHere"}}</p>

		{{!-- THE FOOT, under a hairline, its one button at the right: the same foot the
		      Expeditions card carries with Run beside it, so the two cards end the same way.

		      THE ONE CONTROL THAT DIFFERS BETWEEN THE TWO LISTS, and one button rather than two
		      because it is one act read in either direction: the tick files an encounter down into
		      the Completed fold, and the arrow on a filed one lifts it back onto the live list. The
		      GLYPH turns with the words, so a card says which way it is about to go.

		      IN THE BODY, AND SO ONLY ON AN OPEN CARD. In the head it was a bare tick two pixels
		      from a delete button, on every row of a shut column. Down here it is reached only by
		      opening the card it is about to file. It wears its words; the long form of both
		      directions stays as the tooltip. --}}
		<div class="stonetop-gm-card-foot">
			<button type="button" class="stonetop-gm-encounter-used"
			        data-tooltip="{{#if used}}{{localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.encounters.markUnused"}}{{else}}{{localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.encounters.markUsed"}}{{/if}}"
			        data-tooltip-direction="UP">
				<i class="fas {{#if used}}fa-rotate-left{{else}}fa-check{{/if}}"></i> {{#if used}}{{localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.encounters.markUnusedShort"}}{{else}}{{localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.encounters.markUsedShort"}}{{/if}}
			</button>
		</div>
	</div>
</li>
